From 19474cbd359bb8cb2e8745d981ebe417332d4687 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 10:51:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 13/57] meson: ask for posix_memalign with its declaring header
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

cc.has_function() tries a real link first and, when that fails, falls
back to __has_builtin(). The fallback is guarded — "in case the user
provides a header, including the header didn't lead to the function
being defined ... we assume the builtin is not functional" — but the
guard is switched off when the call passes no prefix, because Meson has
no header to have consulted. So a bare has_function('posix_memalign')
answers YES on any toolchain whose compiler knows the builtin and whose
C library does not implement the function. The comment this replaces
names MinGW as that toolchain and works around it by excluding an OS.

Passing the declaring header turns the guard back on, so the check
reports what the C library has rather than what the compiler knows, and
it does so on every platform: MinGW gets the right answer from the
mechanism instead of from a name, MSVC is already answered NO by Meson
before the builtin fallback runs, and every platform that really has
posix_memalign is unaffected because its link test succeeds and the
fallback is never reached.

util/os_memory_aligned.h already carries a complete os_malloc_aligned()
for platforms without it, so a correct NO costs an aligned-allocation
implementation, not a build failure.

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 4 item 3 (memory objects — the libc gaps the
  first executable link meets)
Why: devkitA64's newlib declares posix_memalign in <stdlib.h>
  (stdlib.h:290) and defines it nowhere, while GCC 15.2.0 knows
  __builtin_posix_memalign. The check therefore answers YES, -D
  HAVE_POSIX_MEMALIGN reaches every compile, and util/sparse_array.c
  ends up referencing a symbol that does not exist. A static archive
  resolves nothing, so it surfaces at the first executable link — which
  for this port is the first Vulkan program.
Evidence: build/mesa-probe/meson-logs/meson-log.txt shows both attempts.
  The link test:
    testfile.c:17: undefined reference to `posix_memalign'
    -> 1
  then the builtin fallback with `#if !1 && !defined(posix_memalign)`,
  -> 0, and "Checking for function "posix_memalign" : YES".
  Reproduced by hand from Meson 1.11.2's template
  (mesonbuild/compilers/mixins/clike.py:810-831), same flags:
    no prefix         (what Mesa asks today) -> YES
    prefix <stdlib.h> (what it should ask)   -> NO
  and a direct link probe:
    posix_memalign   FAILS: undefined reference to `posix_memalign'
  while aligned_alloc and memalign both link, so the C library is not
  short of aligned allocation — only of this spelling of it.
Upstream: yes — it is a property of the C library, tested through the
  header that declares it, and it removes an OS exclusion rather than
  adding one. It also fixes the MinGW case the existing comment
  describes, on toolchains where that comment's workaround does not
  apply because the OS is not Windows.
---
 meson.build | 17 +++++++++--------
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/meson.build b/meson.build
index ef9c244..858c665 100644
--- a/meson.build
+++ b/meson.build
@@ -1640,14 +1640,15 @@ if cc.has_header_symbol('math.h', 'issignaling',
    pre_args += '-DHAVE_ISSIGNALING'
 endif
 
-# MinGW provides a __builtin_posix_memalign function, but not a posix_memalign.
-# This means that this check will succeed, but then compilation will later
-# fail. MSVC doesn't have this function at all, so only check for it on
-# non-windows platforms.
-if host_machine.system() != 'windows'
-  if cc.has_function('posix_memalign')
-    pre_args += '-DHAVE_POSIX_MEMALIGN'
-  endif
+# Some toolchains provide a __builtin_posix_memalign but no posix_memalign
+# in the C library. Asked without a prefix, has_function() falls back to
+# __has_builtin() when the link fails, so it answers yes on exactly those
+# toolchains and compilation fails later. Asking with the declaring header
+# makes Meson's own guard apply, so the answer is the C library's rather
+# than the compiler's — on every platform, which is why no OS needs
+# excluding here.
+if cc.has_function('posix_memalign', prefix : '#include <stdlib.h>')
+  pre_args += '-DHAVE_POSIX_MEMALIGN'
 endif
 
 if cc.has_member('struct dirent', 'd_type', prefix: '''#include <sys/types.h>
